        "subtype": "urn:entity:artist",
        "popularity": 0.999931187700293,
        "affinity": 0.9585745591093621,
        "properties": {
          "description": "The Strokes are a New York City rock band formed in 1998 that became central to the early-2000s garage rock revival. Their 2001 debut album Is This It earned widespread critical praise and established the band’s spare, melodic guitar-driven sound, Julian Casablancas’s detached vocal style, and a cool-yet-urban aesthetic. Across several albums — including Room on Fire, First Impressions of Earth, Angles, Comedown Machine and The New Abnormal — they balanced retro influences with concise pop hooks and modern production, influencing a generation of indie and alternative rock bands.",
          "image": {
            "url": "https://images.qloo.com/i/1A44C04F-7251-4809-809B-6C885FFD68EB-420x-auto.jpg"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "C8DB6D4F-FE7B-465B-92CA-A472E1184E66",
        "name": "Thom Yorke",
        "type": "urn:entity",
        "subtype": "urn:entity:artist",
        "popularity": 0.9986172004535058,
        "affinity": 0.92735279286189,
        "properties": {
          "description": "Thom Yorke is an English musician, singer and songwriter best known as the principal vocalist, principal songwriter and frontman of Radiohead. Beyond the band he has a solo career and fronted the electronic/experimental project Atoms for Peace; his work fuses melancholic melodies, adventurous electronic production and unconventional song structures. Yorke is noted for his plaintive, breathy falsetto, his interest in technology and politics, and for pushing the boundaries between rock and electronic music.",
          "image": {
            "url": "https://images.qloo.com/i/C8DB6D4F-FE7B-465B-92CA-A472E1184E66-420x-auto.jpg"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "7398A0D8-4BA6-48A8-9969-C27C509A3013",
        "name": "Arcade Fire",
        "type": "urn:entity",
        "subtype": "urn:entity:artist",
        "popularity": 0.9999016967147042,
        "affinity": 0.9212580030659797,
        "properties": {
          "description": "Arcade Fire is a Montreal-based Canadian indie rock band celebrated for large-scale, anthemic songs, lush orchestration, and multi-instrumental arrangements. Emerging in the early 2000s, they blend rock, chamber pop and experimental textures into emotionally direct music and theatrical live performances. Their albums (notably Funeral and The Suburbs) combined intimate songwriting with dramatic crescendos and earned widespread critical and commercial success.",
          "image": {
            "url": "https://images.qloo.com/i/7398A0D8-4BA6-48A8-9969-C27C509A3013-420x-auto.jpg"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "D55AA58B-0DB2-43EF-B3B9-E2D40B215E7E",
        "name": "Interpol",
        "type": "urn:entity",
        "subtype": "urn:entity:artist",
        "popularity": 0.9995543584399924,
        "affinity": 0.9201948705496902,
        "properties": {
          "description": "Interpol is an American rock band formed in Manhattan, New York, in 1997. The core lineup is Paul Banks (vocals, guitar), Daniel Kessler (guitar, backing vocals) and Sam Fogarino (drums). They were a central act in the post-punk revival of the early 2000s, blending staccato basslines, interlocking rhythmic guitars, snare-forward production and baritone-led vocals to create a moody, atmospheric sound. Key albums include Turn On the Bright Lights (2002), Antics (2004), Our Love to Admire (2007), Interpol (2010), El Pintor (2014), Marauder (2018) and The Other Side of Make-Believe (2022). Original bassist Carlos Dengler left in 2010 and has not been permanently replaced; touring and session players have handled bass and keys since.",
          "image": {
            "url": "https://images.qloo.com/i/D55AA58B-0DB2-43EF-B3B9-E2D40B215E7E-420x-auto.jpg"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "354C8BBA-A058-4B4E-B0CA-6619A3F348CD",
        "name": "Death Cab for Cutie",
        "type": "urn:entity",
        "subtype": "urn:entity:artist",
        "popularity": 0.9998951431623512,
        "affinity": 0.9173054161038197,
        "properties": {
          "description": "Death Cab for Cutie is an American indie rock band formed in Bellingham, Washington in 1997. Fronted by singer-songwriter Ben Gibbard, the band blends literate, emotionally direct lyrics with melodic guitar- and piano-based arrangements. They rose from the Pacific Northwest indie scene to international prominence in the 2000s with albums such as Transatlanticism (2003) and Plans (2005), merging intimate singer-songwriter sensibilities with polished alternative-rock production.",
          "image": {
            "url": "https://images.qloo.com/i/354C8BBA-A058-4B4E-B0CA-6619A3F348CD-420x-auto.jpg"
